## 2. Chain of Thought (CoT) & Tree of Knowledge (ToK) Architecture

This pipeline traces how a statement transitions from a single principle to a proven fact using first-principles deduction.

```
[ Root Principle / Single Axiom ] 
       │
       ▼
[ Algebraic Logic Parsing ] (Formulates the core thesis statement)
       │
       ▼
 ───[ Kiwix ZIM Library Lookup ] (RAG Retrieval Engine)
       │
       ├──> [ First Evidence Extracted ] (Verified facts from local archive)
       │
       ▼
[ Chain of Thought / Linear Deductions ]
       │
       ├──> Deduction 1: Synthesize Root Principle + First Evidence
       ├──> Deduction 2: Eliminate logical contradictions (Pure Logic)
       └──> Deduction 3: Resolve algebraic constraints
       │
       ▼
[ Tree of Knowledge Expansion ] (Branching alternative hypotheses)
       │
       ├──> Branch A: [ Valid Core Proof ] ──> (Syllogistic Validation)
       └──> Branch B: [ Contradictory Path ] ──> [ PRUNED / REJECTED ]
       │
       ▼
[ Pure Logic Synthesized Answer ] (Returned to User UI)
```

---

## 3. Operational Mechanics

1. **Zero-Training Axiomatic Parsing:** The Sub-1B model does not store historical facts in its weights; it functions strictly as a grammar translator to build an Abstract Syntax Tree (AST) representing the query.
2. **Deterministic Processing:** The reasoning engine operates on mathematical logic constraints, checking valid syntax structures rather than assigning soft statistical weights.
3. **Detachable Grounding Store:** The local Kiwix ZIM archive acts as a static read-only database. It can be unmounted or hot-swapped without retraining any weights, ensuring zero external data dependencies.
